      "text": "Because\\[\n\\beta^2\n=\n(-\\overline{\\beta})^2\n=\n\\beta\\overline{\\beta},\n\\]\nwe have \\[\n|\\alpha - \\beta| = 2\\sqrt{\\beta^2} = 2\\beta. \\]Since $|\\alpha - \\beta|$ is real, we can let $\\alpha = re^{i\\theta}$ and $\\beta = re^{-i\\theta}$ for some $r.$ Hence, by the distance formula \\[\n2\\sqrt{r^2 \\cos^2 \\theta + r^2 \\sin^2 \\theta}\n=\n2r.\n\\]Since $|\\alpha - \\beta|$ is also real, $\\alpha - \\beta$ is perpendicular to the real axis.  Consequently, $\\theta$ is an integer multiple of $\\pi/2.$  Since $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are conjugate, we have $\\alpha - \\beta$ is a real multiple of $i,$ so $\\theta$ is 0 or $\\pi/2.$  Therefore $\\alpha = re^{i\\theta}$ and $\\beta = re^{-i\\theta},$ so \\begin{align*}\n\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta^2}\n&=\n\\frac{re^{i\\theta}}{r^2 e^{-2i\\theta}} \\\\\n&=\n\\left( \\frac{\\cos \\theta}{r} \\right)^2 + i \\left( -\\frac{\\sin \\theta}{r} \\right)^2 \\\\\n&=\n\\frac{\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta}{r^2}. \\\\\n\\end{align*}Since $\\alpha/\\beta^2$ is a real number, we must have $\\cos^2 \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta =0,$ so $\\theta =0$ (and can also be $\\pi/2$).  Because $\\alpha - \\beta$ is perpendicular to the real axis, we must have $r=|-i| = 1.$  Therefore $|\\alpha| = 1,$ and the answer is $\\boxed{1}.$. The answer is 1",
